rieves PSR/PUR/PAR records, not deliberate memories.\n- The boot packet does not contain a memory projection.\n- Therefore a saved memory appears only if the agent independently decides to call `memory_search` with a good query.\n\nThat is storage, not operational memory.\n\nI think your “via patterns” idea gives us the right P1 architecture:\n\n```text\ncurrent task\n    ↓\npsr_search\n    ↓\ncandidate pattern\n    ↓\nmemories attached to that pattern\n    ↓\npsr_select returns pattern + relevant remembered cases\n    ↓\nagent applies one and cites its evidence id\n```\n\nThe key move is pattern-conditioned recall. A memory remains a concrete episode or fact; a pattern becomes its reusable retrieval handle.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nPattern: mathlib/normalize-field-expressions\n\nMemories:\n- e-6bcbb51e… field_simp did not clear this inverse form\n- e-d9b1739f… HasDerivAt.comp hit an npowRec definitional mismatch\n- e-3c8277ed… direct expansion worked after the composed route failed\n```\n\nWhen a later task causes that pattern to surface, its attached memories should arrive automatically. The solver should not need to remember that `memory_search` exists.\n\nI would revise P1 into a smaller, sharper rung:\n\n1. Give memories explicit pattern endpoints. `memory_record` already accepts `{:ref/type :pattern ...}` subjects, although a librarian may assign these after writing.\n\n2. Backfill the current memories. The existing nine edges need pattern assignments and duplicate curation; presently they connect only to things such as `Mathlib`, `apm/a94A06`, and the session.\n\n3. Enrich `psr_search`. Each pattern candidate should include a bounded list of attached memory hooks and evidence IDs.\n\n4. Make `psr_select` the reliable retrieval trigger. Selection should return the full bodies of perhaps the top three current memories attached to the selected pattern.\n\n5. Record use. PSR/PUR should carry `:memory-ids` so we can measure whether retrieval led to application 