tern is itself a typed result, not a dead end. It produces a policy-hole and an evidence-backed candidate pattern, which enters R6 without earned authority and must survive external trials before promotion.\n\nThe missing mechanism is now explicit: failure to retrieve an adequate pattern is itself a typed result, not a dead end. It produces a policy-hole and an evidence-backed candidate pattern, which enters R6 without earned authority and must survive external trials before promotion.\n\nYou’re right—the earlier outline was catalogue-conservative. It could retrieve, attach, split, or merge patterns, but not originate one.\n\nThe missing mint lane is now:\n\n`no adequate R6 candidate → reason-bearing policy-hole → gather unmatched memories → propose candidate pattern → admit with an unearned prior → trial in a cascade → external outcome → revise/promote/merge/prune`\n\nThe important distinction is that creating a pattern is model expansion. R17’s Bayesian Model Reduction can subsequently test whether the expanded model should be retained, merged, or pruned, but BMR cannot invent the new pattern.\n\nA new candidate carries:\n\n- IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE structure\n- The policy-hole that prompted it\n- Supporting and challenging memory IDs\n- Nearest existing patterns\n- A predicted contextual distinction or outcome that can fail\n- Explicit `candidate` status and an unearned prior\n\nSemantic similarity or repeated memories may nominate it, but cannot promote it. Promotion requires use plus an independently witnessed outcome.\n\nI added this mint lane to all three documents, including a dedicated P1b acceptance test in [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md). The paper still builds successfully.\n\nYou’re right—the earlier outline was catalogue-conservative. It could retrieve, attach, split, or merge patterns, but not originate one.\n\nThe missing mint lane is now:\n\n`no adequate R6 candidate → reason-bearing policy-hole → gather unmatched memories → propose candidate pattern → admit with an unearned prior → trial in a cascade → external outcome → revise/promote/merge/prune`\n\nThe important distinction is that creating a pattern is model expansion. R17’s Bayesian Model Reduction can subsequently test whether the expanded model should be retained, merged, or pruned, but BMR cannot invent the new pattern.\n\nA new candidate carries:\n\n- IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE structure\n- The policy-hole that prompted it\n- Supporting and challenging memory IDs\n- Nearest existing patterns\n- A predicted contextual distinction or outcome that can fail\n- Explicit `candidate` status and an unearned prior\n\nSemantic similarity or repeated memories may nominate it, but cannot promote it. Promotion requires use plus an independently witnessed outcome.\n\nI added this mint lane to all three documents, including a dedicated P1b acceptance test in [M-typed-memories.md](/home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/missions/M-typed-memories.md). The paper still builds successfully.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_du8KU6f1sN8IXNCx8LB4sv0t\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '720,750p' main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":5000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_du8KU6f1sN8IXNCx8LB4sv0t\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: \\\\begin{table}[h] \\\\caption{The zaif routemap: what is landed, and the dependency-ordered remainder.} \\\\label{tab:zaif-routemap} \\\\small \\\\begin{tabular}{p{0.…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_thLynPSDcmn08Hhnh0F7LYDj\", :args \"const patch = \\\"*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Update File: /home/joe/code/futon3c/holes/CODEX-HANDOFF-p0-memory-record.md\\\\n@@\\\\n-The intended next read path is pattern-conditioned:\\\\n+The intended next read path …\"}\n{:kind :patch-apply, :ca