ONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nI think you’ve found a real gap, but it has two layers: the paper has a live pattern-mint route operationally, while its formal AIF account mostly describes selection and reduction over an already-given state/action vocabulary. I’m tracing those two stories against the exact R6, R13, R16, and R17 claims so we can say precisely whether cascades solve it—or merely rearrange existing pieces.\n\nI think you’ve found a real gap, but it has two layers: the paper has a live pattern-mint route operationally, while its formal AIF account mostly describes selection and reduction over an already-given state/action vocabulary. I’m tracing those two stories against the exact R6, R13, R16, and R17 claims so we can say precisely whether cascades solve it—or merely rearrange existing pieces.\n\nYes—there is a real gap in the model as a whole.\n\nThe key terminological trap is that an AIF “generative model” is not automatically a creative generator. It predicts how hidden states and actions produce observations: roughly, “if the world is in state \\(s\\) and I follow policy \\(\\pi\\), what observations should I expect?” It does not, by itself, invent new hidden-state categories, actions, or patterns.\n\nThe paper currently contains three different layers:\n\n1. Operational generative model, R1–R4  \n   Hidden operational states such as blockage, progress, and pattern fit predict tests, traces, diffs, and feedback.\n\n2. Policy construction, R6/R13  \n   Existing patterns are action primitives; cascades compose them into policies. Cascades provide combinatorial novelty, but only from known pieces.\n\n3. Model-structure learning, R17  \n   BMR merges or prunes concepts after evidence accumulates. This simplifies an existing model; it does not invent a missing concept.\n\nWhat’s absent from the formal catalogue is model expansion: how a previously unavailable action primitive or state category becomes representable.\n\nThere is, however, already a live route in the paper. The preregistered loop says that fold boxes which cannot be grounded become `policy-holes`, which route to a mint lane; new candidate patterns enter at a uniform prior and earn status through witnessed outcomes. It even reports one double-witnessed candidate in that lane. See [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:703).\n\nSo the mechanism exists operationally, but the catalogue never gives it a first-class R-pattern. Instead, it says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here,” while R6 assumes a bounded set of existing pattern IDs. That is the formal hole.\n\nI think the repaired architecture is:\n\n```text\nFast loop:   fixed current model\nR1–R6 → cascade → fold → act → R16 outcome\n\n                          policy-hole / persistent prediction error\n                                           ↓\nSlow loop:                    candidate pattern generation\n                                           ↓\n                         uniform-prior candidate in R6\n                                           ↓\n                           witnessed trials and review\n                                           ↓\n                    retain / revise / merge / prune via R17\n                                           ↓\n                              updated current model\n```\n\nThe missing catalogue pattern could be something like “Pattern Genesis from Policy-Holes”:\n\n- If R6 repeatedly has no adequate action or a fold exposes an ungrounded step;\n- however cascades can only compose existing patterns and BMR can only reduce existing structure;\n- then bundle the policy-hole, circumstances, failed candidates, memories, and a falsifiable expected outcome into a candidate pattern, admitting it at an unearned prior;\n- because a learning system must sometimes expand the vocabulary in which it represents possible action.\n\nThat also clarifies the status transition:\n\n`memory → evidence for a policy-hole → candidate pattern → trialled pattern → established pattern`\n\nThe paper’s existing definition already says a candidate becomes a pattern only after repeated enactment and review ([main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:424)). What is missing is the formal account of how the candidate is born.\n\nI would make this a first-class node between R16 and R17—or perhaps the missing R18, after checking the historical use of that number. R17 would then be the disposer of proposed structure, not its sole generator.\n\nYes—there is a real gap in the model as a whole.\n\nThe key terminological trap is that an AIF “generative model” is not automatically a creative generator. It predicts how hidden states and actions produce observations: roughly, “if the world is in state \\(s\\) and I follow policy \\(\\pi\\), what observations should I expect?” It does not, by itself, invent new hidden-state categories, actions, or patterns.\n\nThe paper currently contains three different layers:\n\n1. Operational generative model, R1–R4  \n   Hidden operational states such as blockage, progress, and pattern fit predict tests, traces, diffs, and feedback.\n\n2. Policy construction, R6/R13  \n   Existing patterns are action primitives; cascades compose them into policies. Cascades provide combinatorial novelty, but only from known pieces.\n\n3. Model-structure learning, R17  \n   BMR merges or prunes concepts after evidence accumulates. This simplifies an existing model; it does not invent a missing concept.\n\nWhat’s absent from the formal catalogue is model expansion: how a previously unavailable action primitive or state category becomes representable.\n\nThere is, however, already a live route in the paper. The preregistered loop says that fold boxes which cannot be grounded become `policy-holes`, which route to a mint lane; new candidate patterns enter at a uniform prior and earn status through witnessed outcomes. It even reports one double-witnessed candidate in that lane. See [main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:703).\n\nSo the mechanism exists operationally, but the catalogue never gives it a first-class R-pattern. Instead, it says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here,” while R6 assumes a bounded set of existing pattern IDs. That is the formal hole.\n\nI think the repaired architecture is:\n\n```text\nFast loop:   fixed current model\nR1–R6 → cascade → fold → act → R16 outcome\n\n                          policy-hole / persistent prediction error\n                                           ↓\nSlow loop:                    candidate pattern generation\n                                           ↓\n                         uniform-prior candidate in R6\n                                           ↓\n                           witnessed trials and review\n                                           ↓\n                    retain / revise / merge / prune via R17\n                                           ↓\n                              updated current model\n```\n\nThe missing catalogue pattern could be something like “Pattern Genesis from Policy-Holes”:\n\n- If R6 repeatedly has no adequate action or a fold exposes an ungrounded step;\n- however cascades can only compose existing patterns and BMR can only reduce existing structure;\n- then bundle the policy-hole, circumstances, failed candidates, memories, and a falsifiable expected outcome into a candidate pattern, admitting it at an unearned prior;\n- because a learning system must sometimes expand the vocabulary in which it represents possible action.\n\nThat also clarifies the status transition:\n\n`memory → evidence for a policy-hole → candidate pattern → trialled pattern → established pattern`\n\nThe paper’s existing definition already says a candidate becomes a pattern only after repeated enactment and review ([main-2026.tex](/home/joe/code/p4ng/main-2026.tex:424)). What is missing is the formal account of how the candidate is born.\n\nI would make this a first-class node between R16 and R17—or perhaps the missing R18, after checking the historical use of that number. R17 would then be the disposer of proposed structure, not its sole generator.\n\nTOOL DIGEST\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_RtMh1Tug7XRPOwRY25lF4gsa\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n -A12 -B5 'Candidate Pattern Action Space|policy-hole|mint lane|mint|new pattern|candidate pattern|form concepts|generative model|cascade.*R6|R6.*cascade|…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_RtMh1Tug7XRPOwRY25lF4gsa\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 1.3 seconds Output: Warning: truncated output (original token count: 16027) Total output lines: 214 Warning: truncated output (original token count: 17474) Total output line…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_M1iQs9MF0jWa43VDAhFAiawu\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '493,528p' main-2026.tex\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":9000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_M1iQs9MF0jWa43VDAhFAiawu\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: \\\\paragraph{No self-certification.} No self-certification means the system cannot count evidence it manufactured as if it were independent proof. This is …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_NeZPjeCm1FdU7LLp31Vigyfh\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n '\\\\\\\\(R18\\\\\\\\)|\\\\\\\\(R19\\\\\\\\)|R18|R19' main-2026.tex | head -40\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":5000}); text(r.output) \"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_NeZPjeCm1FdU7LLp31Vigyfh\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: 551:\\\\paragraph{Interoceptive Tripwires (R20).}\\\\label{pat:r20} \\\\textbf{If} you have hardened the loop's constructed core (R1--R16) and want the machinery …\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_bAeEIEGT1npLas9nrXl5BJwb\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"rg -n 'R18|R19|pattern mint|mint lane|policy-hole' . --glob '!main-2026.*' --glob '!*.pdf' --glob '!*.svg' | head -120\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_bAeEIEGT1npLas9nrXl5BJwb\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.1 seconds Output: ./questions-for-codex-2.md:74:terminals, and policy-holes\\\" and is checked by \\\"Clojure/Babashka gates\\\" and a ./empirics.tex:18:The live scheduler adds two…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call, :name \"exec\", :call-id \"call_ZdDE09T24m0AJpsR5f5huyya\", :args \"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\\\"sed -n '285,410p' sequel-notebook.org; sed -n '130,180p' NOTE-box1-residuals.md\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"/home/joe/code/p4ng\\\",\\\"yield_time_ms\\\":10000,\\\"max_output_tokens\\\":1400…\"}\n{:kind :custom_tool_call_output, :call-id \"call_ZdDE09T24m0AJpsR5f5huyya\", :output \"Script completed Wall time 0.2 seconds Output: | 4 | ~30% mechanical ceiling; want-text lifts F | Q-B: mean ΔF +0.093, ~29.5% F>0 | DONE | | 5 | Precision is a learned posterior (γ) | E5 futon2 e86802…\"}"}, :session-id "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :tags [:codex :turn-round :codex-rollout :codex-4], :at "2026-07-23T01:28:16.763Z", :type :coordination, :subject #:ref{:type :agent, :id "codex-4"}, :author "codex-4", :claim-type :step, :id "e-codexroll-019f8b63-t007"} #:evidence{:body {:session-uuid "019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822", :turn-id "019f8c9b-3742-7782-b8e3-7b9748c965a9", :event-seq-range [565 595], :reasoning-encrypted-only-count 6, :tool-digest-count 8, :rollout-file "/home/joe/.codex/sessions/2026/07/22/rollout-2026-07-22T20-53-09-019f8b63-a009-79e0-9a22-e7402848c822.jsonl", :turn 8, :event :turn-round, :truncated? false, :message-count 6, :harvester-version 1, :reasoning-count 6, :profile :codex-rollout, :text "Codex rollout turn 8\n\nREASONING (plaintext available in rollout)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAGENT MESSAGES\nGood—then we should treat that explainer as the prior ontology and avoid casually reusing R18. I’ll trace what R18 meant there, how R17 and the seeds/mint route were wired around it, and where the paper diverged from that authoritative summary.\n\nGood—then we should treat that explainer as the prior ontology and avoid casually reusing R18. I’ll trace what R18 meant there, how R17 and the seeds/mint route were wired around it, and where the paper diverged from that authoritative summary.\n\nThe authoritative explainer sharpens this considerably: R17 was originally the broad “model expansion / niche” node—explicitly “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action; the model grows.” R18 was the meta-audit over every other quantity. The paper later narrowed R17 to BMR, so the expansion half fell out of the formal account even though the operational mint/seeds route survived elsewhere. I’m locating that surviving route now.\n\nThe authoritative explainer sharpens this considerably: R17 was originally the broad “model expansion / niche” node—explicitly “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action; the model grows.” R18 was the meta-audit over every other quantity. The paper later narrowed R17 to BMR, so the expansion half fell out of the formal account even though the operational mint/seeds route survived elsewhere. I’m locating that surviving route now.\n\nYes. Tracing from the authoritative explainer changes the diagnosis.\n\nR17 already owned pattern creation. R18 should remain faithfulness.\n\nIn the explainer:\n\n- R17 is explicitly “Structure learning — model expansion / niche.”\n- Its concept says: “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action—the model grows.”\n- Its promise says the War Machine could “invent new action-classes when its current repertoire can’t cope.”\n- R18 is explicitly a meta-level “Faithfulness lens,” auditing whether quantities bearing AIF names actually compute those quantities.\n\nSee [aif-wiring-explainer.html](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/aif-wiring-explainer.html:142).\n\nSo the original conceptual allocation was already right. The problem arose because the implementation description under R17 concentrated on BMR, and the paper later narrowed R17 almost entirely to reduction.\n\nThe historical mechanism\n\nThe operational record reveals a fairly concrete pattern-creation loop:\n\n```text\ncircumstance\n    ↓\nretrieve existing patterns\n    ↓\nconstruct cascade\n    ↓\nthin cascade or fold policy-hole\n    ↓\nlibrary-gap finding\n    ↓\nauthor candidate seed\n    ↓\ningest into embeddings + phylogeny + prior\n    ↓\ncandidate becomes eligible for future cascades\n    ↓\nenactment and external outcome\n    ↓\nearn, revise, merge, or prune\n```\n\nThat is not speculative. The pipeline wiring contains live edges:\n\n```text\ncascade.library-gap-findings\n    → authoring.library-gap-findings\nauthoring.authored-seeds\n    → pattern-library.authored-seeds\n```\n\nSee [wm-pipeline-wiring.edn](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/wm-pipeline-wiring.edn:22).\n\nThe seed work also discovered that authoring a pattern was insufficient. A new pattern had to cross three gates before it could influence action:\n\n1. It had to enter the embedding index, so retrieval could find it.\n2. It had to enter the phylogeny, so cascade construction considered it eligible.\n3. It needed an honest prior, because zero historical use otherwise imposed a prohibitive complexity cost.\n\nThat is documented in [E-live-loop-1-s2-escrow-design.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/E-live-loop-1-s2-escrow-design.md:159).\n\nThe later slush experiment then names essentially the same mechanism the “mint lane”:\n\n`policy-hole → candidate-pattern/open → uniform prior → flown outcomes → library status`\n\nIt even records a worked precedent and one double-witnessed candidate. See [SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/labs/slush-demo/SPEC-full-loop-gfn.md:82).\n\nWhat cascades do—and don’t do\n\nCascades provide compositional novelty. They can create a new policy from known patterns.\n\nThey cannot create a missing primitive.\n\nTheir role in pattern genesis is diagnostic: when a cascade is thin, disconnected, or produces a policy-hole, it provides evidence that the present action vocabulary is inadequate. The cascade is therefore both:\n\n- a policy assembled from the current model; and\n- a sensor for where that model needs expansion.\n\nThe authoring/mint lane performs the expansion.\n\nWhere the paper drifted\n\nThe paper currently has all the pieces, but they are separated:\n\n- The opening definition says candidates become patterns through repeated enactment and review.\n- R6 selects from a bounded set of existing pattern IDs.\n- R13 composes existing patterns into cascades.\n- The experiment describes policy-holes entering a mint lane.\n- R17 formalizes BMR reduction.\n- The catalogue introduction says the underlying pattern collection is “not detailed here.”\n\nThat last gap is exactly where the authoritative R17 story disappeared.\n\nThe clean repair is not a new R-number. It is to restore R17’s original breadth:\n\n| R17 phase | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Expansion | Policy-holes and unexplained memories generate candidate patterns |\n| Admission | Candidates enter the action space at an explicitly unearned prior |\n| Testing | Cascades enact them; R16 supplies independent outcomes |\n| Consolidation | Evidence updates standing; BMR merges or prunes redundant structure |\n\nBMR is therefore the disposer, not the whole of R17. Embeddings, memories, policy-holes, and authoring are candidate generators. R17 names the complete structure-learning cycle.\n\nAnd R18?\n\nIt is slightly strange because it is not part of the generative model. But the R-numbers already mix core inference and supporting apparatus: trace, scheduling, validation, hierarchy, and so forth. R18 is best described explicitly as a meta-pattern or audit lens over the loop, not another latent state or inference operator.\n\nThe paper’s faithfulness badges and retract table are effectively the current realization of R18, even though its dedicated catalogue entry has disappeared.\n\nSo my present conclusion is:\n\n- There is no missing number.\n- There is a missing half of R17 in the paper.\n- The live system has a partially operational expansion route.\n- Cascades expose demand for new patterns but do not themselves author them.\n- Memory-pattern work gives that original R17 route a much better evidence supply.\n\nYes. Tracing from the authoritative explainer changes the diagnosis.\n\nR17 already owned pattern creation. R18 should remain faithfulness.\n\nIn the explainer:\n\n- R17 is explicitly “Structure learning — model expansion / niche.”\n- Its concept says: “new hidden causes, new policies, new kinds of action—the model grows.”\n- Its promise says the War Machine could “invent new action-classes when its current repertoire can’t cope.”\n- R18 is explicitly a meta-level “Faithfulness lens,” auditing whether quantities bearing AIF names actually compute those quantities.\n\nSee [aif-wiring-explainer.html](/home/joe/code/futon2/holes/aif-wiring-explainer.html:142).\n\nSo the original conceptual allocation was already right. The problem arose because the implementation description under R17 concentrated on BMR, and the paper later narrowed R17 almost entirely to reduction.\n\nThe historical mechanism\n\nThe operational record reveals a fairly concrete pattern-creation loop:\n\n```text\ncircumstance\n    ↓\nretrieve existing patterns\n    ↓\nconstruct cascade\n    ↓\nthin cascade or fold policy-hole\n    ↓\nlibrary-gap finding\n    ↓\nauthor candidate seed\n    ↓\ningest into embeddings + phylogeny + prior\n    ↓\ncandidate becomes eligible for future cascades\n    ↓\nenactment and external