—policy priors, model expansion, and structure learning—so we don’t overstate what standard AIF actually supplies.\n\nYes—the retract should not be “design patterns are canonical AIF objects.” It should be: a pattern occupies the implementation role of a policy primitive, and R17‴ addresses expansion of the policy/control-state alphabet when the current generative model cannot express an adequate policy. I’m checking the primary AIF literature for the most exact theoretical home—policy priors, model expansion, and structure learning—so we don’t overstate what standard AIF actually supplies.\n\nYes. The theoretical retract is stronger than “AIF has structure learning somewhere.” R17‴ retracts to expansion of the generative model’s control-state and policy space.\n\nThe crucial distinction is:\n\n- Cascades create new combinations within a fixed action alphabet.\n- Pattern minting expands the alphabet itself.\n\nIn standard discrete AIF, \\(U\\) is the set of actions or “control states,” while each policy \\(\\pi\\) is an allowable sequence of those actions. The \\(E\\) vector assigns a prior to each already-allowable policy. Standard policy inference therefore selects and learns confidence over a supplied repertoire; it does not ordinarily synthesize a new elementary control state. [This AIF tutorial](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8956124/) explicitly distinguishes control states \\(U\\), allowable policy sequences \\(V\\), and the learned prior \\(E\\).\n\nOur correspondence is therefore approximately:\n\n\\[\n\\text{pattern }p \\longleftrightarrow \\text{elementary or temporally extended control }u\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{cascade }(p_1,\\ldots,p_n) \\longleftrightarrow \\text{policy }\\pi\n\\]\n\nR17‴ changes the model from:\n\n\\[\nU' = U \\cup \\{u^\\ast\\}\n\\]\n\nand must also supply beliefs about the new control’s consequences:\n\n\\[\nB' = B \\cup \\{B^{u^\\ast}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThat expands the induced policy space:\n\n\\[\n\\Pi_H(U)\\subset\\Pi_H(U').\n\\]\n\nThis is why a candidate pattern needs more than attractive prose. Until it states a predicted transition or outcome, the forward model cannot imagine its consequences and R5 cannot score policies containing it. The pattern’s THEN clause, expected result, and falsifier are the beginnings of \\(B^{u^\\ast}\\); the fold turns that qualitative transition claim into a concrete construction and forecast.\n\nThe AIF theoretical home\n\nThe closest established home is structure learning by model expansion and subsequent model comparison. Active-inference concept-learning work explicitly expands a model’s state space to accommodate novel causes, after which evidence determines whether the added structure is useful. [Smith et al.’s structure-learning model](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7250191/) is an example, and [Neacsu’s thesis](https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10192997/) distinguishes active inference, parametric learning, and structure learning as separate levels.\n\nOur move from new hidden states to new control states is an implementation-level extension, but the retract is clear:\n\n```text\nImplementation                         AIF theory\n\npolicy-hole / unmatched memories   →   poor evidence under current model\ncandidate pattern p*               →   proposed expanded model m*\npredicted use and outcome          →   new transition hypothesis B^{u*}\nuniform/unearned admission         →   broad prior over expanded alternative\ntrial in a cascade                 →   epistemic policy testing m versus m*\nexternal outcome                   →   evidence under competing structures\npromote / merge / prune            →   model selection / structure learning\n```\n\nThe precise residual—the reason for gray—is candidate generation.\n\nAIF gives us a principled account of:\n\n- representing an expanded candidate model;\n- selecting actions that discriminate between model structures;\n- accumulating evidence;\n- retaining or reducing structure.\n\nIt does not give us a canonical operator that writes a human-legible IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE pattern from memories and policy-holes. That proposal operator is ours: Librarian analysis, LLM authoring, clustering, and pattern-language discipline.\n\nSo the retract-table row could read:\n\n| Mechanism | AIF role | Residual |\n|---|---|---|\n| R17‴ candidate-pattern mint | Generative-model expansion over control states, followed by active model selection | AIF evaluates supplied structural alternatives but does not specify how to synthesize a new human-legible control schema from episodic evidence |\n\nThere is another subtle residual around \\(E\\). In textbook AIF, \\(E\\) has one entry per complete allowable policy, not one entry per elementary action. When we say a new pattern enters “at the uniform prior,” we are really assigning an engineering prior to the pattern and using it to induce priors over newly available cascades. We should not pretend this is literally appending one cell to canonical \\(E\\) unless the cascade set is explicitly enumerated.\n\nR17‴ in the loop\n\nThe complete slow loop would be:\n\n```text\nR6/R13: search policies over the current pattern alphabet\n                       ↓\n          policy-hole or persistent misfit\n                       ↓\nR17‴: propose an expanded control alphabet\n                       ↓\n      candidate pattern with predicted consequences\n                       ↓\nR6/R13: construct discriminating cascades containing it\n                       ↓\nR16: obtain independently witnessed outcomes\n                       ↓\nR3/R7: update beliefs and precision\n                       ↓\nR17: compare, retain, merge, or prune model structure\n```\n\nR15 provides the timescale separation: the fast solver acts within the current vocabulary, while the slow Librarian changes that vocabulary.\n\nAnd the self-evidencing point is lovely but needs the R9 caveat: our inability to express the minting move was genuine evidence that the theoretical vocabulary was incomplete. It warrants proposing R17‴. It does not, by itself, certify that our proposed mechanism is good. R17‴ earns pattern status through the same future enactment-and-review cycle it prescribes.\n\nSo I would classify it as gray but with a very definite retract:\n\n> R17‴ is the War Machine’s engineering realization of active-inference model expansion at the policy/control-state boundary. Its non-canonical residue is the synthesis of the candidate itself.\n\nYes. The theoretical retract is stronger than “AIF has structure learning somewhere.” R17‴ retracts to expansion of the generative model’s control-state and policy space.\n\nThe crucial distinction is:\n\n- Cascades create new combinations within a fixed action alphabet.\n- Pattern minting expands the alphabet itself.\n\nIn standard discrete AIF, \\(U\\) is the set of actions or “control states,” while each policy \\(\\pi\\) is an allowable sequence of those actions. The \\(E\\) vector assigns a prior to each already-allowable policy. Standard policy inference therefore selects and learns confidence over a supplied repertoire; it does not ordinarily synthesize a new elementary control state. [This AIF tutorial](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8956124/) explicitly distinguishes control states \\(U\\), allowable policy sequences \\(V\\), and the learned prior \\(E\\).\n\nOur correspondence is therefore approximately:\n\n\\[\n\\text{pattern }p \\longleftrightarrow \\text{elementary or temporally extended control }u\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{cascade }(p_1,\\ldots,p_n) \\longleftrightarrow \\text{policy }\\pi\n\\]\n\nR17‴ changes the model from:\n\n\\[\nU' = U \\cup \\{u^\\ast\\}\n\\]\n\nand must also supply beliefs about the new control’s consequences:\n\n\\[\nB' = B \\cup \\{B^{u^\\ast}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThat expands the induced policy space:\n\n\\[\n\\Pi_H(U)\\subset\\Pi_H(U').\n\\]\n\nThis is why a candidate pattern needs more than attractive prose. Until it states a predicted transitio