reconstruct or revise such a posterior. I’m checking whether the paper currently makes that boundary consistently elsewhere, especially around observations, traces, and structure learning.\n\nThe distinction emerging is temporal and functional: a belief state is the live posterior used to control the next action; a memory is durable evidence or a reviewed claim that may later help reconstruct or revise such a posterior. I’m checking whether the paper currently makes that boundary consistently elsewhere, especially around observations, traces, and structure learning.\n\nYour two questions identify a real junction, but belief, observation, trace, and memory should remain distinct.\n\nThe short version is:\n\n| Object | Function | Timescale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Observation \\(o_t\\) | Typed evidence received now | One tick |\n| Belief \\(q(s_t)\\), currently called \\(\\mu\\) | Current posterior inferred from evidence | Carried tick to tick |\n| Trace | Persistent record of observations, beliefs, decisions, and model versions | Complete operational history |\n| Memory | Selected, provenance-bearing episode or assertion worth making retrievable | Sparse cross-session history |\n| Pattern | Reusable control schema and retrieval address for relevant memories | Cross-situation structure |\n\nFor line 446, a memory is not simply an old belief. A belief state is the controller’s present probability distribution over hidden states. In the WM implementation, for example, it maps each entity to probabilities over statuses such as `:refined`, `:addressed`, or `:reopened`. It is revised by observations and carried into the next tick.\n\nA memory is a durable record saying, roughly, “an author asserted or experienced this, under these circumstances.” It may preserve something once believed, but it may equally preserve:\n\n- a failed attempt;\n- a counterexample;\n- a volatile fact;\n- a useful technique;\n- a superseded assertion;\n- an unresolved question.\n\nThus its warrant is not “likely to remain true.” It is “potentially informative in a later context.” Its provenance and witness status determine what epistemic weight it should receive.\n\nThe critical AIF connection occurs at recall. Retrieving a memory makes it part of the current evidence available to the agent. But the observation is initially only:\n\n> “The store contains this provenance-bearing record.”\n\nIt is not automatically:\n\n> “The content of this record is true.”\n\nThe recalled memory can inform the current posterior, candidate-policy construction, or expected outcomes. Only subsequent independent evidence should update reliability or establish value. This is exactly why Phase 1 kept the memories `:self-asserted`, and why Phase 2’s use and outcome receipts matter.\n\nThere are consequently two different persistence routes:\n\n\\[\no_t \\longrightarrow q(s_t) \\longrightarrow q(s_{t+1})\n\\]\n\nis ordinary belief filtering, while\n\n\\[\n\\text{episode/assertion}\n\\longrightarrow \\text{durable memory}\n\\longrightarrow \\text{pattern-conditioned recall}\n\\longrightarrow \\text{new }o_{t+k}\n\\]\n\nis long-range, selective recurrence. Memory lets evidence leave one agent’s context window and later re-enter another agent’s inference loop.\n\nFor line 449, your intuition is also right, with one qualification: an observation vector makes comparison over time possible, but does not by itself establish shared context. A single vector is one coordinate; the persistent trace supplies the timeline.\n\nRaw prose works within one context window because the model can interpret it locally. Across sessions, however, prose alone lacks stable coordinates: which subject, event type, time, domain, author, model version, or decision did it concern? Typed observations make these records joinable and comparable. The prose can remain as the inspectable payload, while the typed envelope supplies identity and structure.\n\nThe different durable mechanisms then divide the work:\n\n- The trace is systematic: it records every relevant controller tick.\n- The carried belief is compressed: it summarizes what the controller presently infers.\n- Memory is selective: it preserves episodes judged useful for later retrieval.\n- Patterns organize recall: they say when particular memories may be relevant.\n- Use/outcome receipts reconnect recalled history to later action and independently observed results.\n\nSo I would not describe the observation vector itself as shared context. I would say that typed observations are the atoms from which a durable shared context can be reconstructed. The trace preserves their sequence; the belief state summarizes their current inferential effect; memories selectively carry important episodes across contexts.\n\nOne further issue exposed by the questions: the glossary currently describes \\(\\mu\\) partly as a vector of means and variances, whereas the production WM belief state is now a categorical posterior \\(q(s_t)\\). We should probably present \\(q(s_t)\\) as the canonical object and say that \\(\\mu\\) denotes a continuous/Laplace summary where that representation is used. Otherwise the glossary risks crossing precisely the wires you noticed.\n\nYour two questions identify a real junction, but belief, observation, trace, and memory should remain distinct.\n\nThe short version is:\n\n| Object | Function | Timescale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Observation \\(o_t\\) | Typed evidence received now | One tick |\n| Belief \\(q(s_t)\\), currently called \\(\\mu\\) | Current posterior inferred from evidence | Carried tick to tick |\n| Trace | Persistent record of observations, beliefs, decisions, and model versions | Complete operational history |\n| Memory | Selected, provenance-bearing episode or assertion worth making retrievable | Sparse cross-session history |\n| Pattern | Reusable control schema and retrieval address for relevant memories | Cross-situation structure |\n\nFor line 446, a memory is not simply an old belief. A belief 